+## 11. Staging, arguing with no one, and writing for the wrong reader
+
+Model prose signals importance instead of adding a fact. These patterns are the strongest and most durable tells, because they survive word swaps and word lists date quickly. One sighting is enough to justify an edit. The pattern list and the fixes below follow the taxonomy of Wikipedia's "Signs of AI writing" and the humanizer skill (see `NOTICE`); the wording and examples here are our own.
+
+**Staged contrast.** "Not X but Y," "not just X, it's Y," "this doesn't mean X, it means Y," a clipped negative tail like ", no guessing." The negative half names something nobody claimed, so the positive half sounds bigger. Say the point directly. Keep a contrast only when the negative half corrects a belief the reader really holds, or when both halves carry information. It can span two sentences or a whole paragraph.
+Before: This isn't just a scheduling tool. It's a way to give your week back.
+After: The tool schedules your week for you.
+(Say what the draft says it does; do not decide what "give your week back" should become.)
+
+**One-line closers and fragment rows.** A single-sentence paragraph that repeats the one before ("That is the real win."), the same closer after every section, a sentence that names the lesson of an example the reader just read, a row of fragments ("No tracking. No ads. No catch."). Cut the repeat. Merge the fragments into one sentence with a claim in it. Keep a short line only if it adds a fact or a consequence.
+
+**Sayings that sound deep.** "At its core," "the real question is," "what really matters," "the language of trust," "X is the currency of Y." Replace the saying with the claim it was dressing up. If there is no claim underneath, cut it.
+
+**Staged run-ups.** "Let's dive in," "here's what you need to know," "Honestly?" as a standalone opener, "Here's the thing." Remove the announcement and start with the point. An "honestly" inside a casual sentence is ordinary; the tell is the standalone beat before a routine claim.
+
+**Arguing with no one.** "I'm not saying…," "To be clear," "Don't get me wrong," "a tempting approach would be…," when no objection or option has appeared. It is usually a leftover from an earlier draft. Remove the defense; if it held a real claim, state the claim. Keep an objection the text attributes and answers.
+
+**Repeated sentence openings.** Three sentences in a row starting with the same subject ("She noted… She noted… She filed…"). Merge them or change the subject. Do not ban the word; repetition on purpose for rhythm is fine.
+
+**Writing about the document.** "This section covers…," "the table below compares…," an account of how the text was assembled or sourced, mention of a previous version outside a changelog. Cut it. Keep a caveat that changes what the reader should do, and keep a source credit the reader can follow.
+
+**Writing for a reader who has no context, when theirs is full.** In a thread reply or a review comment, the reader knows the background. A reply that restates the problem, walks through the diagnosis, and puts the decision last re-explains what they already have. Lead with the decision, keep the one fact they lack and any link they need, and leave the full write-up for the ticket or doc. Act on this only when the surrounding conversation is visible or the text is plainly a reply. Say in the flags what you cut.
+
+**Weak signals** (act only with company): a single dash, curly quotes, a hyphenated modifier after the noun ("the report is high-quality"), passive voice, one hedge, "associated with" for a relationship the draft actually states.
 
